ertically until it touches either the top edge or the bottom edge. So the real question is which objects go up and which go down.\n\n**Treat each shape and its loose dots as one object.** In pair 1 the 3×3 block of 2 has two single 2-cells touching its corners diagonally, and all three move together. So I group cells of the same colour using diagonal connectivity. That makes each scattered shape one object, and size means its total cell count.\n\n**Compare sizes with direction.**\n- **Pair 1:** colour 3 has 7 cells and goes up; colour 1 has 7 cells and goes up. Colour 4 has 5 cells and goes down; colour 2 has 11 cells (9 + 1 + 1) and goes down.\n- **Pair 2:** colours 3 and 2 have 9 cells each and go up. Colours 7 and 9 have 3 cells each and go up. Colour 4 has 5 cells and goes down; colour 1 has 4 cells and goes down.\n\nColour does not decide it: colour 2 goes down in pair 1 but up in pair 2, and colour 1 does the opposite. Starting height and shape orientation did not give a consistent pattern either. Size does: **an object whose size is shared with another object rises to the top edge; an object with a unique size sinks to the bottom edge.** This holds for all 12 objects in the training pairs. I have not run it on the grids before submitting, so the verifier's run on the train pairs is the actual check.\n\n**Applying it to the test inputs.**\n- **Test 1:**\n  - Colour 2 (7 cells) and the small colour-3 cross (7 cells) share a size, so both go up.\n  - The colour-3 ring plus its four corner dots has 12 cells, which is unique, so it goes down.\n  - Colour 4 has 6 cells (unique), so it goes down.\n  - Colour 5 has a ring plus three dots, 11 cells (unique), so it goes down.\n- **Test 2:**\n  - Colour 1 (7 cells) and colour 6 (7 cells) go up.\n  - Colour 7 (3 cells) and colour 8 (3 cells) go up.\n  - Colour 4 (5 cells) goes down.\n  - The diamond of 3s has 4 cells (unique), so it goes down.\n\n**How the program builds the output.** It starts from the input and erases every object to the background colour. It then re-stamps each object shifted vertically so that its top row lands on row 0 (if it rises) or its bottom row lands on the last row (if it sinks). After all the risers are placed and after all the sinkers are placed, it emits a checkpoint.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True)\n    from collections import Counter\n    cnt = Counter(o[\"size\"] for o in objs)\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Erase all objects; each will slide vertically to an edge.\")\n    ups = [o for o in objs if cnt[o[\"size\"]] > 1]\n    downs = [o for o in objs if cnt[o[\"size\"]] == 1]\n    for o in ups:\n        y0 = o[\"box\"][0]\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y - y0, x] = g[y, x]\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Objects whose size is shared by another object rise to the top edge.\")\n    for o in downs:\n        y1 = o[\"box\"][2]\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y + (H - 1 - y1), x] = g[y, x]\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Objects with a unique size sink to the bottom edge.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So I group cells of the same colour using diagonal connectivity."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":" does: **an object whose size is shared with another object rises to the top edge; an object with a unique size sinks to the bottom edge.** This holds for all 12 objects in the training pairs."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Objects with a unique size sink to the bottom edge."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every object in these grids keeps its exact shape and column and slides vertically until it touches either the top edge or the bottom edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It then re-stamps each object shifted vertically so that its top row lands on row 0 (if it rises) or its bottom row lands on the last row (if it sinks)."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Erase all objects; each will slide vertically to an edge."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It then re-stamps each object shifted vertically so that its top row lands on row 0 (if it rises) or its bottom row lands on the last row (if it sinks)."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That makes each scattered shape one object, and size means its total cell count."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"87546d43361383797a91cd18bce16ad155adf6c92a681ea084b6ad9e101044df","task_sha256":"90d3010ba4b5027979ffca56972e013a3d08f5682f7e6b1358720c3881b55be3","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
